@Tutorial(time: 50) {
    @Intro(title: "Advanced Rule Patterns") {
        Explore advanced ATL rule patterns including lazy rules, called rules, and imperative sections.

        In this tutorial, you'll learn when and how to use different rule types for sophisticated transformation scenarios.

        You'll discover how lazy rules enable on-demand element creation, how they differ from matched rules in the model they produce, and how called rules provide explicit control over transformation flow.

        The tutorial gives you the source metamodel and the transformations. Writing the target metamodel, creating a sample project and running the transformations with swift-atl are exercises for you: by now you have done each of them in an earlier tutorial, and the hints along the way tell you where to look. Once you have tried an exercise, you can compare your work with the example solution in the section "Example Solutions" at the end, which shows every file, every swift-atl command with its output, and every report.

        @Image(source: "atl-transformations.svg", alt: "ATL Transformations")
    }

    @Section(title: "Lazy Rules") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Lazy rules create target elements only when explicitly requested. Unlike matched rules that automatically apply to all matching source elements, lazy rules provide on-demand element creation.

            Lazy rules are defined with the `lazy rule` keywords and invoked like an operation of the module, as in `thisModule.TaskSummary(task)`, usually from a binding of another rule. Each call returns the target element that the call created.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create the source metamodel, which describes projects.

                @Code(name: "Project.ecore", file: atl-04-step-01-project-metamodel.ecore)

                A Project contains its Tasks and Resources. A Task has a name, a priority and a completed flag, and a Resource has a name and a type.
            }

            @Step {
                Define a basic lazy rule.

                @Code(name: "ProjectTransform.atl", file: atl-04-step-02-lazy-rule.atl)

                Lazy rules only execute when explicitly called, useful for optional or conditional transformations. Nothing calls this rule yet, so running the transformation now would write an empty report.
            }

            @Step {
                Call the lazy rule from a matched rule.

                @Code(name: "ProjectTransform.atl", file: atl-04-step-03-calling-lazy.atl)

                The matched rule HighPriorityProject runs for the project. Its `summaries` binding selects the tasks with a priority above 5 and calls `thisModule.TaskSummary(task)` for each of them, so only those tasks get a Summary.
            }

            @Step {
                Exercise: write the target metamodel, `Report.ecore`, yourself.

                The transformation creates elements of the Report metamodel, which is yours to write. Derive it from the rules: every `Report!X` in a `to` pattern is a class X, and every binding `feature <- expression` is a feature of that class. The expression tells you the type of the feature: a string literal or a name gives an `EString`, a priority an `EInt`, and a collection such as the result of `collect` makes the feature many-valued. An element that a rule creates for a binding of another element, such as a Summary for the `summaries` of a Document, is usually contained by that element, so `summaries` is a containment reference. Give the package the name `Report`, as in the `-- @path` directive, and save it beside the transformation. Start with what these rules need, and extend the metamodel as later steps add classes and features. <doc:01-first-metamodel> shows how to write and validate a metamodel, and <doc:03-metamodel-relationships> how to declare containment and other references. An example solution is in the section "Example Solutions" at the end of this tutorial.
            }

            @Step {
                Exercise: create a sample project and run the transformation on it.

                Write a small model of Project.ecore to transform, as in <doc:02-model-instances>. A good test input has tasks on both sides of the priority threshold of 5 and at least one completed task, for example three tasks with the priorities 8, 6 and 2, of which the first is completed, and two resources. Then run the transformation as in <doc:01-first-transformation>, with a command of the form `swift-atl transform ProjectTransform.atl --source IN=project.xmi --target OUT=report.xmi`. For that example, the report holds one Document with two Summaries, for the tasks with the priorities 8 and 6, with the statuses `Done` and `Pending`. The task with the priority 2 was never passed to the lazy rule, so no Summary exists for it. An example solution is in the section "Example Solutions" at the end of this tutorial.
            }

            @Step {
                Create multiple lazy rules and choose between them for each task.

                @Code(name: "ProjectTransform.atl", file: atl-04-step-06-multiple-lazy.atl)

                Different lazy rules handle different transformation scenarios on demand. The matched rule now summarises every task, calling DetailedTaskSummary for a high-priority task and TaskSummary for the others. Add the class DetailedSummary to your metamodel: its elements go into `summaries`, so it must be a subclass of Summary, as <doc:04-advanced-features> explains. Run the transformation again. For the example project, the report has three summaries: the two high-priority tasks become DetailedSummary elements, marked `xsi:type="rep:DetailedSummary"` and carrying their priority and description, and the task with the priority 2 becomes a plain Summary.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Lazy Rules Compared with Matched Rules") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A matched rule runs once for every source element that its pattern and guard accept, whether or not anything refers to the result. When a binding of another rule names a source element, ATL replaces it with the target element that the matched rule created for it.

            A lazy rule runs only when it is called, and every call creates new target elements. A `unique lazy rule` creates its targets on the first call for a source element and returns the same targets on every later call.

            Try each of the following transformations on your sample project, writing each report to a file of its own so that you can compare them. Each step says what you should observe.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Write the intent of the first lazy version, a report that summarises the high-priority tasks, with a matched rule instead.

                @Code(name: "ProjectTransformMatched.atl", file: atl-04-step-07-matched-rule.atl)

                TaskSummary is now an ordinary matched rule, and the `summaries` binding passes the selected tasks themselves rather than calling a rule. ATL resolves each task to the Summary that TaskSummary created for it. Try it: the Document contains the same two summaries as before, but the matched rule also created a Summary for the task with the priority 2, which nothing asked for. That Summary is contained in nothing, so the report has a second root element beside the Document. To avoid it with a matched rule, you would repeat the selection as a guard of TaskSummary, `(s.priority > 5)`, and keep the two conditions in step. With a lazy rule, the caller alone decides which tasks are summarised.
            }

            @Step {
                Call a lazy rule twice for the same tasks: once for the summaries of all tasks, and again for the highlights.

                @Code(name: "ProjectHighlights.atl", file: atl-04-step-08-lazy-called-twice.atl)

                The `highlights` of a Document should refer to summaries that the Document already contains, so add `highlights` to your metamodel as a many-valued reference to Summary that is not a containment reference. Try it: every call of a lazy rule creates a new element, so the report has five summaries rather than three. The Document contains one for each task, and each high-priority task has a second Summary, a copy that the highlights refer to. The copies are contained in nothing, so they are root elements of the report.
            }

            @Step {
                Declare the lazy rule as `unique`.

                @Code(name: "ProjectHighlights.atl", file: atl-04-step-09-unique-lazy.atl)

                Only the first line of the rule changes. A unique lazy rule remembers the elements that it created for each source element, so the second call for a task returns the Summary of the first call. Try it: the report has three summaries, all inside the Document, and the highlights refer to the first two of them, as in `<highlights href="#//@summaries.0"/>`. A matched rule gives the same structure when two bindings name the same task, because both resolve to the one Summary that it created. Use a plain lazy rule when each call should produce a separate element, a unique lazy rule when calls for the same element should share one result, and a matched rule when every element of a type needs a result.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Called Rules and Imperative Sections") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Called rules are explicitly invoked procedures that can create multiple target elements and execute imperative code. They provide procedural control flow within declarative transformations.

            The `do` block enables imperative operations like assignments, conditional statements and loops. Its statements end with a semicolon, and an imperative `if` takes its condition in parentheses and its branches in braces.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Define a called rule.

                @Code(name: "ProjectTransform.atl", file: atl-04-step-10-called-rule.atl)

                Called rules are invoked explicitly and can accept parameters. Unlike a matched rule, a called rule has no `from` section, so it only runs when it is called: with only this rule, the transformation writes an empty report. The rule creates a StatusReport, another class for your metamodel.
            }

            @Step {
                Add an imperative do block.

                @Code(name: "ProjectTransform.atl", file: atl-04-step-11-do-block.atl)

                The `using` section declares local variables of the rule. The `do` block contains imperative code that executes after the target element has been created and its bindings applied, so it can assign `t.completionRate`. The operator `div` divides two Integers and drops the remainder, which suits an integer attribute; `/` would give a Real.
            }

            @Step {
                Combine called rules with matched rules.

                @Code(name: "ProjectTransform.atl", file: atl-04-step-12-combined-rules.atl)

                The matched rule calls the lazy rule from a binding and the called rule from its `do` block. The StatusReport that the called rule creates is not contained in the Document, so it becomes a root element of the report.
            }

            @Step {
                Build a complete transformation with advanced patterns.

                @Code(name: "ProjectTransform.atl", file: atl-04-step-13-complete-transformation.atl)

                The complete transformation demonstrates lazy rules, called rules, and imperative sections working together. It needs every class and feature of your metamodel, including the ResourceItems that the second lazy rule creates for the `resources` of a Document.
            }

            @Step {
                Exercise: check your metamodel with the complete transformation.

                Run the complete transformation on your sample project. For the example project, with the tasks of priority 8 (completed), 6 and 2 and two resources, a correct metamodel gives a report with two root elements. The Document has the completion rate 33 and the status message `Project in progress: 33% complete`, and contains two Summaries, for the tasks with a priority of at least 3, each with its priority level, and two ResourceItems with their categories. Beside it, the StatusReport records 3 tasks, a completion of 33 and 2 resources. If your metamodel lacks a feature or a class, swift-atl stops with a message such as `Transformation failed: Invalid operation: Property 'statusMessage' not found in class 'Document'` or `Transformation failed: Type error: Class 'ResourceItem' not found in model 'OUT'`. If the summaries appear as root elements that the Document only refers to, as in `<summaries href="#/1"/>`, the `summaries` reference is not a containment reference. An example solution is in the section "Example Solutions" at the end of this tutorial.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Example Solutions") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            This section works through an example solution of each exercise of this tutorial, in the order in which the tutorial poses them. Try each exercise yourself first, then compare your files, commands and reports with the ones shown here.

            The steps build the target metamodel, `Report.ecore`, in the same small increments in which the tutorial asks for it, and run each transformation on one sample project, in the directory that holds `Project.ecore` and your transformations. Each run first shows the version of the transformation that it uses, as you may have moved on to a later version since.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Write the target metamodel that the first rules need.

                @Code(name: "Report.ecore", file: atl-04-step-15-report-metamodel.ecore)

                The rules of the step that calls the lazy rule create a Document with a `name` and `summaries`, and a Summary with a `title` and a `status`. All three attributes take strings, so they are `EString` attributes. The `summaries` binding collects several Summaries that belong to the Document, so it is a containment reference with the upper bound -1, which makes it many-valued. The package name `Report` matches the `-- @path` directive; the namespace URI and the prefix `rep`, which appears in the reports below, are your choice.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the sample project.

                @Code(name: "project.xmi", file: atl-04-step-16-sample-project.xmi)

                The project Garden Shed has three tasks with the priorities 8, 6 and 2, of which the first is completed, and two resources. The tasks and resources are the contents of the Project, so they are written as child elements named after the containment references `tasks` and `resources`.
            }

            @Step {
                Check both files with swift-ecore.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: atl-04-step-17-validate.sh)

                `swift-ecore validate` checks that a metamodel is well formed and, with `--metamodel`, that a model conforms to its metamodel.
            }

            @Step {
                Take the transformation that calls the lazy rule for the high-priority tasks.

                @Code(name: "ProjectTransform.atl", file: atl-04-step-03-calling-lazy.atl)

                This is the version from the step that calls the lazy rule from a matched rule. If you have moved on to a later version, save this one as `ProjectTransform.atl` again, so that the run below writes the report that this section shows.
            }

            @Step {
                Run the transformation on the sample project.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: atl-04-step-19-run-calling-lazy.sh)

                `--source IN=project.xmi` binds the model IN of the `create` line to the sample project, and `--target OUT=report.xmi` names the file that receives the model OUT. swift-atl finds `Project.ecore` and `Report.ecore` beside the transformation through its `-- @path` directives.
            }

            @Step {
                Open the report that the transformation wrote.

                @Code(name: "report.xmi", file: atl-04-step-20-report-calling-lazy.xmi)

                The Document contains one Summary for each of the two tasks with a priority above 5. The task Paint trim, with the priority 2, was never passed to the lazy rule, so the report has no Summary for it.
            }

            @Step {
                Add the class DetailedSummary to the metamodel.

                @Code(name: "Report.ecore", file: atl-04-step-21-report-detailed-summary.ecore)

                DetailedSummary names Summary in `eSuperTypes`, so it inherits `title` and `status`, and a DetailedSummary can be an element of `summaries`, whose type is Summary. Its own attributes are the two that only DetailedTaskSummary binds: `priorityLevel`, an `EInt` because it takes the priority of a task, and `description`, an `EString`.
            }

            @Step {
                Take the transformation with the two lazy rules.

                @Code(name: "ProjectTransform.atl", file: atl-04-step-06-multiple-lazy.atl)

                The matched rule now summarises every task, and chooses DetailedTaskSummary for a task with a priority above 5.
            }

            @Step {
                Check the extended metamodel and run the transformation again.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: atl-04-step-23-run-multiple-lazy.sh)

                The run replaces `report.xmi` with the new report.
            }

            @Step {
                Open the new report.

                @Code(name: "report.xmi", file: atl-04-step-24-report-multiple-lazy.xmi)

                Every task now has a summary. The two high-priority tasks became DetailedSummary elements, which carry their priority and description. As the type of `summaries` is Summary, `xsi:type="rep:DetailedSummary"` records the subclass of each. Paint trim became a plain Summary.
            }

            @Step {
                Take the version of the first report that uses a matched rule instead of the lazy rule.

                @Code(name: "ProjectTransformMatched.atl", file: atl-04-step-07-matched-rule.atl)

                It creates the same kinds of elements as before, so the metamodel needs no change.
            }

            @Step {
                Run it into a report of its own.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: atl-04-step-26-run-matched.sh)

                Writing to `report-matched.xmi` leaves `report.xmi` as it is, for comparison.
            }

            @Step {
                Compare the report of the matched rule with the first report.

                @Code(name: "report-matched.xmi", file: atl-04-step-27-report-matched.xmi)

                The Document contains the same two summaries as the first report. The matched rule also created a Summary for Paint trim, which nothing contains, so it is a second root element, and the report wraps its two root elements in an `xmi:XMI` element.
            }

            @Step {
                Add the `highlights` reference to the metamodel.

                @Code(name: "Report.ecore", file: atl-04-step-28-report-highlights.ecore)

                The highlights refer to Summaries that `summaries` already contains. An element has only one container, so `highlights` is a many-valued reference to Summary without `containment`.
            }

            @Step {
                Take the transformation that calls the lazy rule twice.

                @Code(name: "ProjectHighlights.atl", file: atl-04-step-08-lazy-called-twice.atl)

                The `summaries` binding calls TaskSummary for every task, and the `highlights` binding calls it again for each high-priority task.
            }

            @Step {
                Check the metamodel and run the transformation.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: atl-04-step-30-run-highlights.sh)

                This report goes to `report-highlights.xmi`.
            }

            @Step {
                Open the report with the highlights.

                @Code(name: "report-highlights.xmi", file: atl-04-step-31-report-highlights.xmi)

                The report has five summaries. The Document contains one for each task, and the second calls created two more, for Pour slab and Frame walls, which are root elements beside the Document. The highlights refer to these copies, `#/1` and `#/2`, the second and third root elements, rather than to the summaries inside the Document.
            }

            @Step {
                Declare the lazy rule as `unique`.

                @Code(name: "ProjectHighlights.atl", file: atl-04-step-09-unique-lazy.atl)

                Only the first line of the rule changes.
            }

            @Step {
                Run the unique version into a report of its own.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: atl-04-step-33-run-unique.sh)

                Writing to `report-unique.xmi` keeps both versions of the highlights report for comparison.
            }

            @Step {
                Compare the report of the unique lazy rule with the previous one.

                @Code(name: "report-unique.xmi", file: atl-04-step-34-report-unique.xmi)

                The report has three summaries, all inside the Document, which is the only root element again. The highlights refer to the first two summaries of the Document, `#//@summaries.0` and `#//@summaries.1`, which summarise Pour slab and Frame walls.
            }

            @Step {
                Add the class StatusReport and the attribute `completionRate` for the called rules.

                @Code(name: "Report.ecore", file: atl-04-step-35-report-status.ecore)

                The called rule CreateStatusReport binds `projectName`, a string, and `totalTasks`, an Integer. The `do` block of the step on imperative code assigns `completionRate` to the Document, so Document gains an `EInt` attribute. Nothing contains a StatusReport, so no reference refers to it. This version suffices for the first three steps of "Called Rules and Imperative Sections".
            }

            @Step {
                Complete the metamodel for the complete transformation.

                @Code(name: "Report.ecore", file: atl-04-step-36-report-complete.ecore)

                The complete transformation also binds `completion` and `resourceCount` of a StatusReport, `statusMessage` of a Document, and `resources`, which contains the ResourceItems, each with a `name` and a `category`. Its lazy rule TaskSummary binds `priorityLevel` of a plain Summary, so the attribute moves up from DetailedSummary to Summary, from which DetailedSummary still inherits it.
            }

            @Step {
                Take the complete transformation.

                @Code(name: "ProjectTransform.atl", file: atl-04-step-13-complete-transformation.atl)

                This is the version from the last step of "Called Rules and Imperative Sections".
            }

            @Step {
                Check the complete metamodel and run the complete transformation.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: atl-04-step-38-run-complete.sh)

                If your own metamodel makes the run stop with a message about a missing property or class, compare it with the complete metamodel above.
            }

            @Step {
                Open the complete report.

                @Code(name: "report.xmi", file: atl-04-step-39-report-complete.xmi)

                The Document has the completion rate 33 and its status message, and contains the summaries of the two tasks with a priority of at least 3, each with its priority level, and the two resource items. The StatusReport that the called rule created is the second root element, and records 3 tasks, a completion of 33 and 2 resources.
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            When should you use a lazy rule instead of a matched rule?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                When target elements should only be created on demand

                @Justification(reaction: "Correct!") {
                    Lazy rules only execute when explicitly invoked, whereas matched rules automatically process all matching elements. The matched version of the high-priority report also created a Summary for every task with a lower priority, which nothing asked for.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                When you want faster transformation

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite!") {
                    Performance isn't the main consideration. Lazy rules provide control over when elements are created.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                When working with large models

                @Justification(reaction: "Incorrect!") {
                    Model size doesn't determine rule type. Lazy rules are about conditional creation, not optimisation.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            A binding calls the lazy rule `TaskSummary` for a task that an earlier binding has already passed to it. What does the second call return?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                The Summary that the first call created

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite!") {
                    That is what a `unique lazy rule` does. A plain lazy rule keeps no record of its earlier calls.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                A new Summary for the same task

                @Justification(reaction: "That's right!") {
                    Every call of a lazy rule creates new target elements. That is why the highlights of the plain lazy version referred to copies of the summaries rather than to the summaries inside the Document.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Nothing, because a lazy rule runs at most once for each source element

                @Justification(reaction: "Incorrect!") {
                    A lazy rule runs every time it is called. Only a matched rule runs once for each source element, and only a unique lazy rule returns its earlier result.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            What is the purpose of a called rule?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                To automatically match source elements

                @Justification(reaction: "Try again!") {
                    Matched rules handle automatic matching. Called rules are explicitly invoked like functions.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                To provide explicit procedural control over transformation

                @Justification(reaction: "That's right!") {
                    Called rules act like functions that can be explicitly invoked with parameters, providing procedural control within declarative transformations.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                To replace helper functions

                @Justification(reaction: "Not correct!") {
                    Called rules create target elements and can contain imperative code. Helpers compute values without side effects.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            What can you do in a `do` block that you can't do in bindings?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Create new target elements

                @Justification(reaction: "Try again!") {
                    Both can lead to new target elements: a binding can call a lazy rule, and a `do` block can call a called rule. The target elements of the rule itself are created by its `to` section before the `do` block runs.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Execute imperative statements like assignments and loops

                @Justification(reaction: "Exactly!") {
                    The `do` block allows imperative operations including assignments, conditional statements, and iteration that aren't available in declarative bindings.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Call helper functions

                @Justification(reaction: "Incorrect!") {
                    You can call helpers in both bindings and `do` blocks. The distinguishing feature is imperative operations.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
